  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Sunday)\nIssued at 1204 AM CDT MON AUG 24 2026\n\nThe radar is clear across Central Alabama late this evening with\nonly a few storms near the Gulf Coast. Our surface boundary of\nlate has progressed today to the southern third of Alabama and\nhas stalled. It is expected to linger in this area through the\nshort term through early Tuesday. In the upper levels, a low is\npresent over Ontario and Quebec and southward into the Great\nLakes and much of Eastern Conus. At the same time, there is also\na large ridge that extends from Arizona to Texas. This leaves\nCentral Alabama in a northwest flow regime. Look for isolated to\nscattered waves of convection at times through Monday, generally\nin the southern third of Central Alabama with heating, more so\ntoward the coast. On Tuesday, the surface boundary is expected to\nmeander back northward before fizzling out on Wednesday. However,\nthe northwest flow will assist in another front making it into\nthe area in the Thursday/Friday time frame. This will keep\nunsettled conditions along with scattered slightly higher storm\nchances in through the latter part of the forecast as moisture\nincreases. Guidance with this late week front has it likely\nstalling across Central Alabama as well next weekend.\n\nThrough much of the 7 day forecast, a few upper lows rotate\nthrough the current trough area over Eastern Canada and the Great\nLakes with varying degrees of southward progression into Eastern\nConus, but overall some degree of troughing remains through the\nend of this week. Our Southwest US ridge remains as well until\nFriday. The difference from yesterday's guidance is that we could\nsee an expansion of the ridge eastward across the Southern US\nthis weekend. This will mean higher pops Wednesday into Friday,\nbut by next weekend we could see a warming trend if the ridge\nexpands.\n\n08",
